My Approach to the Next Role

Over the years, I have built strong technical expertise and a strong sense of ownership. One of my strengths has always been that when I take responsibility for something, I stay committed and don't give up easily until I find a solution.

At the same time, as I grow with the organization, I understand that my role needs to evolve beyond individual delivery towards enabling the team, managing risks, and contributing to the broader organizational goals.

I see the next role as an opportunity to use my experience differently — not just to solve problems myself, but to help the team solve problems effectively and consistently.

1. Think beyond the immediate task

One of my key learnings is that solving the immediate technical problem is only one part of delivery.

Before committing to something, I want to understand the bigger picture:

- What is the business objective?
- What are the dependencies?
- What are the risks?
- What is the impact on other teams?
- Do we have the right capacity?
- What happens after implementation?
- Is the solution sustainable and scalable?

This helps me make better decisions rather than focusing only on completing the immediate task.

2. Commit responsibly

I have always had a strong ownership mindset, and sometimes that naturally makes me want to say, “I will get it done.”

One thing I have learned is that ownership should also include understanding capacity, dependencies and uncertainty before making a commitment.

Going forward, I want to make commitments based on proper assessment.

If there is uncertainty, I would communicate it early, provide options and align on priorities rather than overcommit.

For me, being accountable doesn't mean saying yes to everything; it means making a commitment that I can stand behind.

3. Deliver through the team

Another important learning is that as responsibilities grow, I cannot measure my success by how much I personally deliver.

I need to create ownership within the team.

That means:

Delegate → Provide clarity → Enable → Remove blockers → Review → Recognize

I would still remain accountable for the overall outcome, but I want the team members to own their areas and grow through that ownership.

My objective is not to become the person who solves every problem.

My objective is to build a team that can solve problems effectively even when I am not directly involved.

4. Communication is part of delivery

I have also learned that how we communicate is as important as what we communicate, particularly when working with senior stakeholders and cross-functional teams.

Earlier, when I was focused on solving an urgent problem, my communication could sometimes become very direct.

I received feedback on this, and I took it positively.

I have consciously worked on making my communication more structured, respectful and solution-oriented.

Today, instead of simply communicating that something is blocked, I try to communicate:

What happened → Impact → Risk → Options → Recommendation → Next steps

This makes the conversation more productive and helps stakeholders make decisions quickly.

I see this as an important part of my growth towards a leadership role.

---

Four Things I Have Learned Recently

Learning 1 — Ownership vs. Over-commitment

I have learned that there is a difference between taking ownership and taking everything on myself.

Ownership means making sure the outcome is achieved.

It does not necessarily mean that I personally have to do every part of it.

As I grow into a leadership role, I want to retain my ownership mindset while making it scalable through the team.

---

Learning 2 — Risk should be managed before it becomes an issue

I have learned to look at risks earlier instead of focusing only on delivery.

When I see a potential risk, I want to understand:

Probability → Impact → Mitigation → Contingency → Communication

For example, if there is a dependency that could affect a delivery date, I would rather highlight it early and work on an alternative than wait until the deadline is close.

I believe early visibility gives the organization more options.

---

Learning 3 — Delegation is development

Earlier, my natural approach was often to take ownership myself because I knew I could get the task completed.

But I have learned that delegation is not simply about distributing work.

It is about creating opportunities for people to take ownership and develop their capabilities.

I want to delegate with clear expectations and accountability, while remaining available to guide and remove blockers.

That creates a stronger and more independent team.

---

Learning 4 — Leadership requires a broader perspective

My technical experience has taught me to look deeply into a problem.

My recent learning is that leadership requires me to zoom out as well as zoom in.

I need to understand the technical details, but also consider:

Business impact + Customer impact + Operational risk + Cost + Security + Availability + Scalability + Long-term maintainability

That broader perspective is something I am continuously developing.

---

How I See Myself in the Next Role

I don't see the next role simply as a designation change.

I see it as a change in responsibility and mindset.

I want to continue using my technical experience, but I want to apply it at a broader level.

I want to be someone who:

- Provides clear direction.
- Makes realistic commitments.
- Identifies and communicates risks early.
- Enables people to take ownership.
- Handles difficult situations calmly.
- Communicates respectfully with stakeholders.
- Thinks beyond the immediate project.
- Connects technical decisions to organizational objectives.
- Continues learning from people around me.

I also believe that experience does not mean that I know everything.

I have significant experience, but I still learn from my colleagues, stakeholders, leadership and even from situations where my initial approach could have been better.

I see that continuous learning as an important part of leadership.

My Closing Statement

I believe I have built a strong foundation through technical expertise, ownership and consistent delivery.

As I grow with the organization, I now want to expand that contribution.

I want to move from being someone who personally drives delivery to someone who enables successful delivery through people, manages risks proactively, communicates effectively and contributes to the broader organizational objectives.

That is the responsibility I am ready to take on in the next role.